AUDIO: 'We trained our own AI solution. We captured around&nbsp; 15 hours of highquality VCON motion captured data.&nbsp;&nbsp; A bunch of performers having to walk slow,&nbsp; walk medium, walk fast, change direction lots&nbsp;&nbsp; um to create a huge data set. And [music] that is&nbsp; Jack Mororrow, co-founder and CEO of Animotive.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Jack built Animotive to take advantage&nbsp; of VR as a production and creative tool,&nbsp;&nbsp; not the final output. He spent years in cinematic&nbsp; VR production delivering 360 project for Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp; Save the Children and major brands before&nbsp; making the pivot. We will look at how animotive&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'practically works and what was their top priority&nbsp; when building the product. Animotive is basically&nbsp;&nbsp; a virtual stage. So if you have performers,&nbsp; camera operators, directors, [music] we want&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them all to be collaborating in the same&nbsp; shared virtual space. We\'ve made it a key&nbsp;&nbsp; focus of our tool that [music] we can move as&nbsp; smoothly as possible uh into Maya, Blender,&nbsp;&nbsp; Unity or Unreal. What we export is all spatially&nbsp; [music] accurate. We look at various use cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'including the one you might not expect. The&nbsp; most popular use of live performance with our&nbsp;&nbsp; tools is actually in museums and discovery [music]&nbsp; centers. It\'s super easy to use our tools to have&nbsp;&nbsp;'

