[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for a number of reasons. Uh one of which is uh&nbsp; eyelines. So, if you have multiple 3D characters,&nbsp;&nbsp; um, and if you have, uh, performers that don\'t&nbsp; match the proportions of the characters they\'re&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to be, which happens, you know, in games&nbsp; in kids animation a lot, um, then, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp; if they\'re going to reach out and pick up a&nbsp; prop, if they\'re going to look at someone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'ve got to see the world from that character\'s&nbsp; perspective and be able to see in truly real time&nbsp;&nbsp; where things are so that their ey lines line up&nbsp; so that they\'re reaching out the right distance&nbsp;&nbsp; for that arm length to grab things. Um so we use&nbsp; VR essentially to uh align everything so that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh we can reduce the cleanup tasks in post. So&nbsp; what happens then just if I understood correctly&nbsp;&nbsp; is that the fact that the actor is within a&nbsp; headset and can perform that at scale then it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'means also that he sees the stage as a virtual&nbsp; stage let\'s say in the headset and then can&nbsp;&nbsp; interact with let\'s say uh objects that are around&nbsp; him and maybe have a physical chair where the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

