VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a screen? Does it happen also in a headset? Like&nbsp; how how will this is this orchestrated? Yeah. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; it it can happen both ways. Um, so people can do&nbsp; it on the desktop or they can do it in VR and do a&nbsp;&nbsp; handheld camera and do things that way. basically&nbsp; like uh and the other weird thing that animation&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provides in this space that people aren\'t used&nbsp; to uh is the ability to work asynchronously. So&nbsp;&nbsp; uh you can go perform character one, perform&nbsp; character two, handheld record camera one,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and layer it all up piece by piece. Um but&nbsp; the most weird part of that is acting against&nbsp;&nbsp; yourself. Um because uh conversations need to be&nbsp; synchronized. Um and so uh we\'ve worked with some&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people who are incredibly good at that. Uh and&nbsp; actually that the category of people who are good&nbsp;&nbsp; at that is puppeteers. Um because they are used&nbsp; to layering up multiple puppets in one shot to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'achieve a final shot. Um [laughter] but uh that\'s&nbsp; not everybody. So, uh, the other workflow we found&nbsp;&nbsp; works incredibly smoothly is, um, when you\'ve&nbsp; pre-edited your audio and you\'re just lining&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up the performances because that\'s what controls,&nbsp; uh, the pace and synchronization at the same makes&nbsp;&nbsp; sense. So then let\'s say that you have let\'s say&nbsp; recorded one or two characters in a headset. You&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have the face uh, being recorded, right? It means&nbsp; eye position, mouth, uh let\'s say expressions,&nbsp;&nbsp; we have the body. Oh, and actually also forgot&nbsp; what about hands and fingers, right? Is there&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything there? Can I without the controllers?&nbsp; Uh at the moment we just do uh different poses&nbsp;&nbsp; uh for the hands that are controlled with the&nbsp; controllers. So you can set like point, pinch,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh fist, thumbs up, um which control like it does&nbsp; a lot of the use cases, but it\'s not everything.&nbsp;&nbsp; Uh so that\'s one of the things we\'re working on&nbsp; at the moment. Um why these features take a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[17:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of time uh is because of uh you know, basically&nbsp; there\'s as many bones in the hands as there&nbsp;&nbsp; is for the rest of the body. Um, and we\'ve got&nbsp; to deal with uh retargeting there as well. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and so that means, you know, if somebody\'s doing&nbsp; hand tracking and they pinch and they have fingers&nbsp;&nbsp; that are different lengths to your fingers,&nbsp; you\'ve got to understand the maths so that,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you know, people are able to do things like that.&nbsp; Uh, otherwise it all gets messy with fingers&nbsp;&nbsp; intersecting and and and I mean again because I\'m&nbsp; ignorant, right? Uh, apologies for that. But how&nbsp;&nbsp; hard it is to get it from the meta itself or are&nbsp; you so that that hand tracking information? Uh so&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[18:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'getting the data is fine. Um getting the data to&nbsp; line up perfectly uh between different characters&nbsp;&nbsp; is hard. Okay, because this brings me to the next&nbsp; point and it\'s the retargeting part, right? So&nbsp;&nbsp;'

