VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a sense like how does this then continues in&nbsp; the workflow? What do I get after? Do I get for&nbsp;&nbsp; example an FBX and how does it integrate in tools&nbsp; like I don\'t know Blender Maya like the one you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mentioned. Yeah. So uh we\'ve made a key focus of&nbsp; our tool that we can move as smoothly as possible&nbsp;&nbsp; uh into Maya Blender Unity or Unreal. Um FBX is uh&nbsp; still the easiest way to do that. Um, and what we&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'export is all spatially accurate uh to each other.&nbsp; So, uh, if you imported characters, sets, props,&nbsp;&nbsp; added animations to the props and characters,&nbsp; uh, that will all export in one FBX file uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'relative to each other. Um, which is surprisingly&nbsp; rare actually in 3D tools. Uh normally you&nbsp;&nbsp; have to go through this whole uh reassembly&nbsp; retargeting process, but because we did the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[19:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retargeting live on the character, um you know,&nbsp; we know that if your character has little legs,&nbsp;&nbsp; uh how many steps it takes to get to the counter&nbsp; like uh so you know, everything is uh comes out&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in one file uh all with the correct relationship&nbsp; to each other. So then wait a second. You made a&nbsp;&nbsp; good point, right? So, let\'s imagine I\'m an adult.&nbsp; I make two steps to go to the counter and then the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'motion is retargeted to let\'s say a a boy, right?&nbsp; Someone or shorter. Would there still be those&nbsp;&nbsp; steps or there is an extra one done in order&nbsp; to compensate for the distance? So, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retarget you to the character um and you would see&nbsp; the world from that character\'s perspective. So,&nbsp;&nbsp; if you are a small boy uh and you as an adult&nbsp; would take uh two steps to get to the counter,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[20:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but he would take three, you\'re going to end&nbsp; up taking three steps in the real world. Um so,&nbsp;&nbsp; so the so the thing is that then I am basically&nbsp; like fooled or let\'s say put in the shoes of my&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character so that when I also behave or perform&nbsp; I am in it. Right. Exactly. And I we did a shot&nbsp;&nbsp; recently where we had six characters with weird&nbsp; and wonderful proportions all walking to screen at&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same time. Uh and this would actually be quite&nbsp; a complicated thing to calculate uh typically. Um,&nbsp;&nbsp; so what we did was we we took the character&nbsp; with the shortest legs, uh, and we recorded that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character first to see in the whole length of this&nbsp; great big studio, how far can he actually walk.&nbsp;&nbsp; Um, he had to set the pace and then the others&nbsp; could line up their pace going forward to that. Um&nbsp;&nbsp; but it became quite an intuitive process. Whereas&nbsp; uh you know that if you were trying to calculate&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[21:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the different stride lengths of the different&nbsp; characters uh to get all of that to line up in&nbsp;&nbsp; time uh the typical way that would take a long&nbsp; time whereas we went start with the short guy&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[22:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'follow them. So of course I mean the purpose and&nbsp; the core is to get motion into let\'s say uh um in&nbsp;&nbsp; 3D right uh of course there is there is I think a&nbsp; different angle here it\'s not just getting motion&nbsp;&nbsp;'

